  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Tenant Screening Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a tenant screening form?\", \"a\": \"A tenant screening form collects what a landlord needs before ordering a report on a renter: pre-screen answers, the applicant's identity details and a signed authorization. The report itself comes from a screening company the landlord chooses.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a free tenant screening form?\", \"a\": \"Write down your screening criteria, then describe the pre-screen questions and the consent page to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>. It builds the form along with a table that holds every applicant. The free tier covers building and publishing it, and unlike a tenant screening form PDF, no answer has to be retyped.\"}, {\"q\": \"How to conduct tenant screening?\", \"a\": \"Post written criteria with the listing and hold every applicant to the same list, in the order they apply. Pre-screen inquiries before showings, then get a signed authorization and order the report from a screening company. Record each decision with the criterion behind it, and send an adverse action notice if the report played any part in a denial.\"}, {\"q\": \"Who pays for tenant screening?\", \"a\": \"Either side can, depending on the screening service. Some bill the landlord for each report, and many landlords pass that cost on as an application fee; others charge the applicant directly. Some states cap the fee. California sets its limit in Civil Code 1950.6 and requires an itemized receipt for what the fee paid for.\"}, {\"q\": \"How far back does a tenant screening go?\", \"a\": \"Seven years for most negative items. Federal law bars consumer reporting agencies from reporting most negative information older than that. Bankruptcies can stay for 10 years, and records of criminal convictions have no federal time limit.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I run a tenant screening report on myself?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Ask the screening companies landlords use for your file. The CFPB keeps a list of them with how to request a report from each. Reading yours before you apply leaves time to dispute anything wrong.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I get a free tenant screening report?\", \"a\": \"Often, yes. Many tenant screening companies give one free copy every 12 months on request. A landlord who denies you or asks for a co-signer because of a report must tell you which company supplied it. You then have 60 days to ask that company for a free copy.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a tenant background check form include?\", \"a\": \"The applicant's legal name and date of birth, recent addresses, a plain statement of which company runs the report, and a signature with the date. Leave out anything about family plans or faith, since the Fair Housing Act protects both. Employers use a separate background check form for job candidates. On Emergent, each signature is stored with its date in a database you own.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the difference between a tenant background check form and a rental application?\", \"a\": \"The rental application asks about the renter, such as where they work and what they earn. The tenant background check form is the consent that lets a screening company pull records on them. Many landlords collect both in one sitting, with the <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/rental-application-form'>rental application form</a> first.\"}, {\"q\": \"What tenant screening questions should you ask before a showing?\", \"a\": \"Ask only what your written criteria test, such as move-in date, the number of people moving in, pets and monthly income against the rent. Questions about plans for children have no place here. An Emergent form can send them to every inquiry and hold the answers until you read them.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
}